{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: ship the mutation harness, and find out why the CLI suite is slow.\n\nWhy. Every lane here ends with a mutation table, and every lane writes its own\nthrowaway script to produce one. Today's ran the whole cmd/gs package for each\nmutation and would have taken about 110 minutes; the same table, run against only\nthe test that should catch each mutation, took 22 seconds. The difference is not\ncleverness, it is that a mutation proof needs one red test and the author already\nknows which one. Three things followed from the slow version that matter more\nthan the minutes:\n\n- Two mutants did not compile, and the script counted them as kills. A mutant\n  that does not build proves nothing about behaviour, and both were hiding a real\n  defect: a branch that could not be tested at all, and startup wiring no test\n  could reach.\n- A killed run left a mutated file in the worktree, and the snapshots taken to\n  restore it were themselves taken from a mutated tree. The lane spent time\n  proving the source was what it claimed to be.\n- Because the table cost an hour, it ran once, at the end. It is worth running\n  whenever a guard changes.\n\nThe underlying cost is separate and larger: cmd/gs takes between five and\nthirteen minutes because its fixtures shell out to git constantly. Nothing here\nfixes that, and it is the reason every lane's last hour is spent waiting.\n\nOutcome. A mutation table is something this repository runs, not something each\nlane reinvents; it is fast enough to run while working; it cannot report a\ncompile failure as a proof, and cannot leave the tree mutated. And the cost of\nthe CLI suite is measured and explained, so the next lane knows whether it is\nworth fixing and what fixing it would take.","body":{"branch":"request/mutation-harness","conditions":"1. The repository holds a mutation harness that takes a table of (guard, file, before, after, the test that should catch it) and reports one line per mutation. It runs only the named test first, and escalates to the whole package exactly when nothing named catches the mutation, which is the only case where a claim of \"no test red\" has to be paid for in full. 2. It distinguishes three outcomes, never two: a named test went red, nothing caught it, and the mutant did not compile. A mutant that does not build is reported as such and never counted as a proof. 3. It restores the tree it mutated even when it is interrupted, and it verifies the restored tree against the source it started from rather than against a snapshot it took of a tree that may already have been mutated. A second run on a tree it left is a no-op. 4. Running the harness against this repository at main produces a table in under two minutes on the machine the report names, and the report states the before and after. 5. The CLI suite cost is measured: how long cmd/gs takes, what fraction of it is git subprocesses, and which fixtures dominate, stated as evidence in docs/reference/performance.md with the machine named. Whether to fix it is a separate decision and this request does not prejudge it. 6. docs/ records how to run the harness and what its three outcomes mean. 7. go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review; ratified; landed on main and pushed.","target_head":"762f34ae6e5cc69ac3724aec3231edca03520479","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}